# Credentials. `scanprivacy scan --sign cosign-key --key <path>` takes a private key,
# and the obvious place to leave one is the directory you ran the command in.
# A signing key in the history is not a mistake you can take back by deleting
# the file, so the ignore has to be here before the first time it matters.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
cosign.key
cosign.pub.pem
data/scans/
reports/
*.sarif
*-badge.svg
scanprivacy.json

# An operator's own measurements. `data/baseline/<date>/` holds kept snapshots
# and `data/fleet-targets.txt` the list of sites to scan — both are the estate
# of whoever runs this, not part of the engine, and this repository is the
# engine. Committing them shipped 46 files nothing references, and a map of one
# person's properties with a grade against each.
#
# They stay on disk. `data/baseline/README.md` is tracked, because the
# convention is worth documenting even though the data is not.
# The glob is deliberately loose. A directory renamed by hand — `mv data/fleet
# data/fleet-old` before a rescan — walked straight out of a narrower pattern
# and the next `git add -A` committed 46 estate files back in, four commits
# after they were removed. Nothing complained. tests/test_repo_hygiene.py now
# fails on a tracked scan result, because a rule enforced only by a filename
# pattern is a rule that holds until somebody types a different filename.
data/baseline/*/
data/fleet*
!data/fleet-targets.txt.example

